# Maintainer: Nidara Project <https://github.com/nidara-project>
#
# nidara-release — the product's identity: /etc/os-release.
#
# ─── WHY THIS IS A PACKAGE AND NOT A LINE IN THE INSTALLER ───────────────────
# A name written by an installer is written once, on the machines that happened
# to be installed that week, and there is no route back to them. This travels by
# `pacman -Syu` instead — which matters less now than it did when this package
# carried a version, and still matters: every field below (the support URLs, the
# ANSI colour, the logo) is a thing we may want to correct on machines that are
# already out there.
#
# It is also why the desktop does NOT ship this. `install.sh` puts Nidara Desktop
# on an Arch somebody already uses, and it must not rename their operating
# system — on that machine About correctly reads "Arch Linux" for the OS and
# "Nidara Desktop" for the environment. Renaming the OS is the PRODUCT's act, so
# nothing depends on this package: the ISO and the installer name it explicitly,
# which is exactly the list of paths that are entitled to.
#
# ─── WHY IT LIVES IN nidara-repo AND NOT IN nidara-iso (moved 2026-08-30) ─────
# It began in nidara-iso, and the reason was the version: `/etc/os-release` said
# `VERSION=0.1.0`, that number WAS the product's, and the only honest way to keep
# it from drifting was to make cutting a product version mean tagging the image
# repo. That coupling bought a lockstep gate in build-repo.sh and a second pin
# (`NIDARA_ISO_REF`) in pins.env.
#
# The version is gone (nidara-iso/PRODUCT.md, "The machine is rolling, the image
# has a date"), and the coupling went with it: nidara-iso is no longer tagged at
# all, so a package fetched from its tags could no longer be published. What is
# left is the rule `nidara-apps` set and `nidara-system` confirmed — **this repo
# holds the packages whose content must change without cutting a release of
# something else**. Fixing a support URL must not require building a 2 GiB image,
# for the same reason a boot default and the app list must not.
#
# ⚠️ It stays SEPARATE from `nidara-system`, which is a different question from
# which repo it sits in, and the answer is bootstrap order: this is what must
# exist BEFORE anything Nidara can be installed (the name, and where Nidara comes
# from), with zero dependencies by obligation; `nidara-system` is what Nidara
# changes ONCE installed, with the dependencies its policy needs. A machine's
# name must not hang off `plymouth` resolving, and uninstalling a boot policy
# that broke your boot must not also un-name your operating system. Ubuntu and
# Fedora split them the same way, and somebody who knows Linux looks for
# `nidara-release`.
#
# ─── /etc/os-release IS UNOWNED, AND STILL NOT FREE (both MEASURED 2026-08-25) ─
# 1. No package owns it. `filesystem` ships only /usr/lib/os-release; systemd
#    reads /etc first when it is there. So we can own it outright — no forked
#    `filesystem` (Manjaro's road) and no pacman hook (EndeavourOS's, which
#    overwrites a file `filesystem` DOES own and leaves `pacman -Qkk filesystem`
#    dirty forever).
# 2. But the path is occupied: systemd's /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/etc.conf drops a
#    symlink there, so on a machine that has booted, installing this hits
#    "error: nidara-release: /etc/os-release exists in filesystem" — pacman's
#    check for an existing UNOWNED path, and it runs before any scriptlet, so no
#    install= hook can clear the way. The installer therefore passes
#    `--overwrite /etc/os-release`, once, for that one path.
#    (Verified by `pacman -U` into a scratch root under fakeroot: with the
#    symlink present it fails; with the path empty it installs. Both arms run.)
# 3. Once the real file is in place it STAYS: that tmpfiles line is `L`, not
#    `L+` — create-if-absent, not force. (`/etc/mtab`, two lines below it, is the
#    `L+` that would have clobbered.) Verified with `systemd-tmpfiles --root=` on
#    a tree holding the real file, against a control where it recreated the link.
#
# No backup=() on purpose. The original reason was that a modified file would
# freeze the product's version; the version is gone and the reason survives it
# unchanged — this file is IDENTITY, not configuration, and the update must win.
# A machine that has edited its own name is not a machine we can answer questions
# about.
#
# ⚠️ `pkgver` tracks NOTHING and is not expected to move. It tracked the product's
# version, which no longer exists; it is now a plain counter, the same as
# `nidara-apps`'. TO CHANGE THIS PACKAGE: edit below and bump `pkgver` — without
# the bump, pacman sees no update and no machine ever gets the change.
pkgname=nidara-release
pkgver=2
pkgrel=1
pkgdesc="Nidara — the operating system's identity and where Nidara comes from"
arch=(any)
url="https://github.com/nidara-project/nidara-iso"
license=(GPL-3.0-only)

# ⚠️ ONE of this package's two files is backed up, and the asymmetry is the
# decision. `/etc/os-release` is IDENTITY: a machine that has renamed itself is
# not one we can answer questions about, so the update must win and there is no
# backup= for it. `/etc/pacman.d/nidara-mirrorlist` is CONFIGURATION: pointing
# pacman at a local mirror or a proxy is a legitimate thing for an admin to do,
# and taking that away on every upgrade would be hostile.
#
# backup= does not weaken what this file is here for. An UNMODIFIED file is still
# replaced by the upgrade — which is the whole point, and the reason the address
# moved out of pacman.conf — while a modified one is kept with a .pacnew beside
# it. The people who lose the new address are exactly the people who chose their
# own.
backup=(etc/pacman.d/nidara-mirrorlist)

package() {
    # ID_LIKE="arch" is the lineage, stated in the field that exists for stating
    # it: scripts branch on it, AUR helpers and `pacman`-aware tools read it, and
    # it is what makes "based on Arch Linux" a machine-readable claim rather than
    # marketing. Rebrand, don't appropriate.
    #
    # ⚠️ NO `VERSION` and NO `VERSION_ID`, and their absence is the decision —
    # `BUILD_ID=rolling` is the whole answer to "what version is this machine?".
    # The defect that removed them: this package carries no `backup=`, so every
    # machine that had ever run `-Syu` reported the newest number published, and
    # a field whose value is identical across the entire fleet informs nobody.
    # Ubuntu's `VERSION_ID` works because it does NOT change — it names the series
    # that machine follows, and Nidara has no series. The full argument is in
    # nidara-iso/PRODUCT.md, "The machine is rolling, the image has a date".
    #
    # Consumers that want a number have three real ones — the versions of
    # `nidara-desktop`, `nidara-system` and `nidara-apps` — and each says
    # something true about a part that actually changes.
    install -Dm644 /dev/stdin "$pkgdir/etc/os-release" <<EOF
NAME="Nidara"
PRETTY_NAME="Nidara"
ID=nidara
ID_LIKE="arch"
BUILD_ID=rolling
ANSI_COLOR="0;36"
HOME_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project"
DOCUMENTATION_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project/nidara-desktop"
SUPPORT_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project/nidara-desktop/issues"
BUG_REPORT_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project/nidara-desktop/issues"
LOGO=nidara
EOF

    # ── where Nidara comes from ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    # The address of this repository, in a file a package owns, so that it can be
    # CORRECTED. It used to live as a bare `Server =` line inside
    # /etc/pacman.conf — a file no package owns and every admin edits — which
    # made the address something only a NEW INSTALL could learn. If this
    # repository ever moves, every machine already out there is stranded on a
    # 404, and the only fix is a sentence in release notes asking people to edit
    # a config file by hand.
    #
    # This is the same shape as Arch's own `pacman-mirrorlist`, and the reason
    # `Include = /etc/pacman.d/mirrorlist` is what every Arch machine has instead
    # of a literal server line.
    #
    # ⚠️ QUOTED heredoc, unlike the one above. `$arch` must reach the file
    # LITERALLY — pacman expands it, we must not. An unquoted heredoc here would
    # interpolate this PKGBUILD's own `arch=(any)` and publish
    # `.../nidara-repo/any`, which 404s on every machine that installs it. The
    # os-release block above is unquoted on purpose, and used to interpolate
    # $pkgver; these two must not be edited into each other's shape.
    #
    # ⚠️ BOOTSTRAP: a pacman.conf whose Include names a file that is not there is
    # not a degraded repo, it is a hard parse error on EVERY pacman invocation —
    # `pacman-key` included, which is how the installer trusts this repo's key in
    # the first place. So the medium ships this file in its own airootfs and the
    # installer writes it to the target BEFORE its first pacman call; this
    # package then adopts it, which is what `--overwrite` is doing on the command
    # that installs this package. (Measured: `pacman-conf` exits 1 with
    # "config file ... could not be read" on a missing Include.)
    install -Dm644 /dev/stdin "$pkgdir/etc/pacman.d/nidara-mirrorlist" <<'EOF'
#
# /etc/pacman.d/nidara-mirrorlist
#
# The Nidara package repository. Referenced from /etc/pacman.conf as:
#
#     [nidara]
#     SigLevel = Required DatabaseOptional
#     Include  = /etc/pacman.d/nidara-mirrorlist
#
# This file belongs to the `nidara-release` package, so a corrected address
# reaches machines that are already installed. Edit it if you run a local mirror
# — your version is preserved across upgrades and the new one arrives as
# `nidara-mirrorlist.pacnew`.
#
Server = https://nidara-project.github.io/nidara-repo/$arch
EOF
}
